  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "four nested loops treats this as one big 4-way combination problem. what if you treated it as two separate 2-way problems instead?",
    "every pair (i from nums1, j from nums2) has a sum. what if you counted how many times each possible sum occurs, using a hash map?",
    "build a hash map of sum -> count for every pair (a, b) with a from nums1 and b from nums2",
    "then for every pair (c, d) with c from nums3 and d from nums4, look up how many times -(c + d) occurred in that map, and add that count to your answer"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "trying to hash all four arrays together instead of splitting into two independent pairs",
    "looking up (c + d) instead of the negation, -(c + d), in the precomputed sum map",
    "not accounting for the count of each sum (treating the map as a set instead of counting duplicates)"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given four separate lists of integers, all the same length.\n\n**Count how many combinations of one index from each list (`i`, `j`, `k`, `l`) make `nums1[i] + nums2[j] + nums3[k] + nums4[l]` equal zero.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "n == nums1.length == nums2.length == nums3.length == nums4.length",
      "0 <= n <= 200",
      "-2^28 <= nums1[i], nums2[i], nums3[i], nums4[i] <= 2^28"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "all lists contain only zero",
      "no combination sums to zero (answer 0)",
      "many repeated values across the lists, producing many valid combinations"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
